**What**
Unit enum variants, `None`, `Unit`, `Null`, and empty collections all encode as
an *empty tree*, so their entire information content lives in a tree entry name
whose subtree holds no blobs. Git list and diff machinery is blob-oriented and
elides these paths completely.

**Where**
`crates/facet-git-tree/src/ser.rs:288-292` (unit variant to empty tree),
`ser.rs:269-273` (`None` to empty tree), `ser.rs:382` (`Null` to empty tree),
`ser.rs:328-336` (variant name as the only entry).

**Why it matters**
Reproduced on a scratch repo with the shipped `schemas/task.json`, changing
`priority` from `Low` to `High`:

```
$ git diff refs/store/task/t1~1 refs/store/task/t1
                      <- completely empty
$ git log --stat -1 refs/store/task/t1
103d165 escalate to High
                      <- completely empty
$ git ls-tree -r refs/store/task/t1^{tree}
100644 blob 02e4a84  done
100644 blob de20668  summary
100644 blob 2e65efe  tags/0000
                      <- `priority` does not appear at all
```

The same happens to schemas, which are themselves mostly unit variants. Changing
a field from `U32` to `String` and running `git diff refs/schema/recipe~1
refs/schema/recipe` produces **zero output**.

This is the demo. `crates/git-store/README.md` promises "git ls-tree, git
cat-file, git log, git diff all work" and "history and blame are per field".
For enums and options - two of the most common shapes in real data - they
show nothing. If one audience member types `git diff` on stage, the central
claim collapses.

**Options**
- Option A - give every empty tree a marker blob (e.g. a zero-length or
  one-byte entry named `_` inside the variant payload tree). `ls-tree -r` and
  `diff` then show `priority/High/_` appearing and `priority/Low/_`
  disappearing. Cost: one extra object per unit value; changes all object ids;
  slightly noisier `ls-tree`.
- Option B - encode a unit variant as a *blob* holding the variant name rather
  than a tree named by the variant. `priority` becomes `100644 blob` containing
  `High`, and the diff reads `-Low` / `+High`. Cleanest possible diff, and it
  matches how a human thinks of an enum. Cost: a unit variant and a
  payload-bearing variant then have different tree shapes, so the reader must
  branch on blob-vs-tree; and it diverges from the uniform external tagging in
  `serialization.design.trees.variants`.
- Option C - leave the encoding alone and ship `git store diff <kind> <name>
  [rev] [rev]` that renders a semantic diff of two decoded values. Cost: the
  pitch quietly becomes "our tool is the query language", not git.

**Recommendation**
Option B for unit variants specifically, plus a marker blob (Option A) for
`None` / `Null` / empty collections. Both are breaking encoding changes, which
is exactly why they should land before the talk rather than after. If that is
too much churn, ship Option C as a safety net and lead the demo with a struct
field change rather than an enum change. High confidence this is the single
most damaging finding for the conference; moderate confidence on which option
is best.